<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lam Sơn</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lam Sơn is a village in Thanh Hóa province in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, the home of the landowner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Lê Lợi</a>. From there he launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lam-son-uprising">Lam Sơn uprising</a> in 1418 against Ming rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaozhi-province">Jiaozhi Province</a>, a ten-year war that ended with the Ming withdrawal in 1427. Lê Lợi then founded the Later Lê dynasty, and the Lam Kinh complex near the village holds the tombs of its early emperors.</p></div>
